meta {
  name: 9d3. Verify api-access Token Reads Protected Named API
  type: http
  seq: 26
}

get {
  url: {{apisixApiUrl}}/v1/datasets/{{sagaDataSetId}}/{{sagaSlug}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{sagaApiAccessToken}}
}

script:pre-request {
  // OPA's authorization data for a freshly released dataset propagates asynchronously and is cached
  // briefly, so an immediate read can transiently 401/403. Poll until it succeeds (max 30s) so the
  // assertions test the access contract, not the propagation race.
  const axios = require('axios');
  const url = `${bru.getEnvVar('apisixApiUrl')}/v1/datasets/${bru.getVar('sagaDataSetId')}/${bru.getVar('sagaSlug')}`;
  const token = bru.getVar('sagaApiAccessToken');

  // Fail loudly rather than poll with an empty bearer — that would burn 30s and then assert against
  // an anonymous 401 that has nothing to do with this test.
  if (!token) {
    throw new Error('sagaApiAccessToken is not set — step 1 must mint an api-access token first');
  }

  // Guard each attempt separately: the QuickJS sandbox does not honour validateStatus across the VM
  // boundary, so a 401/403 rejects the promise and would otherwise abort the whole loop.
  let authorized = false;
  for (let i = 0; i < 15 && !authorized; i++) {
    try {
      const resp = await axios.get(url, {
        headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
        timeout: 10000,
      });
      authorized = resp.status === 200;
    } catch (e) { /* transient 401/403 while OPA data propagates; retry */ }
    if (!authorized) {
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 2000));
    }
  }
  if (!authorized) {
    throw new Error('Protected named-API read never returned 200 within 30s — OPA authorization did not propagate');
  }
}

tests {
  test("api-access bearer reads the protected named API through the gateway (200)", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("Response is the upstream JSON payload", function() {
    expect(res.body).to.be.an('object');
  });
}

docs {
  **The external-consumer contract: a public-client token reads a protected named API.**

  This is the headline named-API test. It sends the `api-access` bearer minted in step 9d2 to
  `{apisixApiUrl}/v1/datasets/{id}/{slug}` and requires 200 with a JSON payload from the upstream. That
  proves the documented contract end to end: an external consumer authenticating against the **public**
  `api-access` Keycloak client — no client secret, no portal session — can read a dataset's named API
  through the gateway while the dataset is still protected (`openDataAccess=false`).

  Compare with step 9c: the same URL, the same route, the same OPA policy — only the credential differs.
  The pair pins that access is decided by the caller's identity, not by a separate "public" route variant.

  The pre-request polls for up to 30 seconds because OPA's authorization attributes for a freshly
  released dataset propagate asynchronously, so an immediate read can transiently be denied.

  **Precondition:** `sagaApiAccessToken` set (step 9d2); `sagaDataSetId` / `sagaSlug` set; the dataset is
  AVAILABLE with `openDataAccess=false`; the token's user holds read access on the dataset.

  **Expected Response:** 200 OK, body is a JSON object from the named API's upstream.

  **System Impact:**

  | Layer | Effect |
  |---|---|
  | Backend | — |
  | Config Adapter | — |
  | External System | Read-only GET against the named API's upstream |
}
